{
  "Reorder rows": "",
  "With `rowDragging: { allowReordering: true }` a column of drag icons comes at the start: take a row by its icon and drag it, a copy of the row follows the pointer and a line shows where it would land — or, with `dropFeedbackMode: 'push'`, the rows move aside to make room for it. Near the top or bottom edge the rows scroll. Drop it and `onReorder` tells where it went:, the grid does not move the data itself, the handler does. Here the rows are in memory: take the row out and splice it in where `toItemData` (the row at `toIndex`) is. Ctrl+Shift+Up and Down move the active row the same way.": "",
  "Reorder rows on a server": "",
  "On a server the order is a field the rows are sorted by. The handler asks the server to move the row to the place of `toItemData` and gives the grid the promise (`e.promise`): the line stays until the answer comes, then `refresh()` loads the rows in their new order. 10,000 rows, the virtual list loading blocks as it scrolls.": "",
  "Drag rows between grids": "",
  "Grids with the same `group` take each other's rows: drop a row on the other grid and `onRemove` comes on the grid it left, `onAdd` on the one it went to (pair), with `toIndex` and `toItemData` saying where. Both grids here push their rows aside and reorder too.": "",
  "rowDragging": "",
  "Rows move within the grid.": "",
  "A row can be dropped onto another (the middle half of a row): `dropInsideItem` in the events, for trees and details.": "",
  "Where a drop would land: a line between the rows, or the rows moving aside to make room for it (not with `allowDropInsideItem`).": "",
  "A column of drag icons, pinned to the start, to drag rows by; off, the whole row is dragged (a click still selects and edits).": "",
  "A CSS selector of what a row is dragged by, instead of the icon or the whole row.": "",
  "Grids of the same group take each other’s rows; `data` is handed to the events as `fromData` / `toData`.": "",
  "Where the copy of the row may go.": "",
  "none, the grid": "",
  "The element the dragged row stays inside; the element its copy is shown in.": "",
  "where the row was taken": "",
  "Where the copy sits from the pointer.": "",
  "a copy of the row": "",
  "Content of your own under the pointer: a component, an element or a text.": "",
  "The rows scroll while the pointer is near their top or bottom edge: pixels per frame at the edge, and how near it starts.": "",
  "The active row moves a place up or down with these keys (any chord: `Mod+Alt+Up`…), with the same events as a drop; announced.": "",
  "Width of the drag icon column, in pixels.": "",
  "How far the pointer moves before a drag starts, and whether a copy follows it; the same as for headers.": "",
  "How dragging does it": "",
  "The grid moves nothing itself (as in the standard grid): `onReorder` and `onAdd` / `onRemove` move the row in your array or tell the server. The grid reads your rows as they are, plain or reactive: after the move call `e.component.refresh()` (rows in memory and a server alike).": "",
  "Rows can be dragged as they are shown; the indexes are the shown ones. A list the user orders by hand usually turns `sortable` off; while grouped the keys move a row within its group, and only data rows are dragged (not group rows, new rows or rows still loading).": "",
  "The rows under the pointer are the pooled rows drawn now; near the edges the rows scroll and the new ones take the marks. Paged, a row moves within its page; the indexes count across pages.": "",
  "The drag icon cell is named “Drag to move the row” with the keys in `aria-keyshortcuts`; drops and key moves are announced politely.": "",
  "rowDragging handler": "",
  "A row is about to be dragged (pointer or keys); `cancel` keeps it in place.": "",
  "The row moved under the pointer; `cancel` takes no drop there.": "",
  "The place it would land changed (another index, another grid, onto a row); `cancel` takes no drop there.": "",
  "The row is about to be dropped (`toIndex` -1 when it is over no grid that takes it); `cancel` drops nothing.": "",
  "Dropped elsewhere in this grid: move it in the data. `promise`: the grid waits for it, the marks shown.": "",
  "Dropped on another grid of the group: `onRemove` on the grid it left, `onAdd` on the one it went to.": "",
  "Field of the event": "",
  "What it holds": "",
  "The grid raising it, the grid the row comes from, the grid under the pointer (the same while reordering, `null` over none).": "",
  "The row dragged, its key, its element.": "",
  "The grid’s row indexes (as `focusedRowIndex` and `getVisibleRows()` count them, across pages too): where the row is, and where it would be after the drop.": "",
  "The row at `toIndex` before the drop — splice the row in where it is; `undefined` at the end of the other grid or on a group row.": "",
  "Dropped onto the row at `toIndex`; the grids’ `rowDragging.data`; the pointer or key event.": "",
  "The four drag events can be cancelled; the three drop events take a promise.": "",
  "`grid.rowDragging.allowReordering = true`, handlers too; `onOptionChanged` tells `rowDragging.allowReordering`.": "",
  "Moves a row as a drop there would: `onDragStart`, `onDragChange`, `onDragEnd` (any can cancel), then `onReorder`; whether it was raised.": "",
  "Moves the active row a place up or down (`allowReordering`); the row stays the active one, and the move is announced (“Row moved to position 3 of 20”).": "",
  "Cancels the drag; nothing is dropped.": ""
}
